From 6d6c439b66efcc9328f2aad6611d0f68843d8f43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Heinemeier Hansson <david@hey.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 22:22:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: Use the performance governor in performance on
 Snapdragon X

The caps method tells the profiles apart by capping scaling_max_freq,
and left the governor at schedutil in every profile. schedutil raises
the frequency only as a task's tracked utilisation grows, over tens of
milliseconds, so bursty work such as a web browser runs most of its
bursts at low clocks even with no cap at all. On a Snapdragon X2 Elite
(ASUS Zenbook A16, X2E-88-100) on AC in performance, Speedometer 3.1
scored 26.5 +/- 4.1 under schedutil and 45.3 +/- 2.6 under the
performance governor, against 48 on Windows on the same machine. Pinning
the browser to the prime cores under schedutil only reached 30.1, and
Google's own Chrome build scored 28.2, so neither core placement nor the
browser build accounts for the gap.

With the caps method on scmi and qcom-cpufreq-hw, switch each policy to
the performance governor in performance and back to schedutil in
balanced and power-saver, in the same pass that writes the caps, so new
and returning policies and resume are covered the same way. A policy
that doesn't offer both governors keeps its own. A failure to switch is
reported but doesn't fail the profile change, since the caps still tell
the profiles apart. When the daemon stops, the governor is set back to
schedutil along with lifting the caps, so the CPUs aren't left at full
clock.

Apple Silicon machines that fall back to the caps method keep their
governor, as this was only measured on Snapdragon X.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
