index 8816992..4fe28c0 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -287,10 +287,16 @@ scmi
 scmi
 ```
 
-The scaling governor and the minimum frequency are not changed. The caps are
-re-applied after resume. When the daemon stops, they are lifted to the
-hardware maximum, so another tool's lower `scaling_max_freq` is not preserved
-(see [Conflicts](#conflicts)).
+The scaling governor follows the profile: `performance` in "performance", and
+`schedutil` in "balanced" and "power-saver", on policies that offer both.
+`schedutil` raises the frequency only as a task's tracked utilisation grows,
+over tens of milliseconds, so bursty work such as a web browser runs most of
+its bursts at low clocks: on a Snapdragon X2 Elite, Speedometer 3.1 scored 26.5
+under `schedutil` and 45.3 under `performance`. The minimum frequency is not
+changed. The caps and the governor are re-applied after resume. When the
+daemon stops, the caps are lifted to the hardware maximum and the governor is
+set back to `schedutil`, so another tool's lower `scaling_max_freq` or other
+governor is not preserved (see [Conflicts](#conflicts)).
 
 A cluster whose CPUs are all offline is skipped, even at startup. When it comes
 back online, or a new policy appears, it keeps the cap it last had until the
